[
  "[SCENE: Host camera. Start conversationally.]\nI have been thinking about what it means to return to an old conversation.\n\nFor years, I recorded interviews with people in the software community. Some of those conversations are more than a decade old. I want to revisit them and hear what is still useful now.\n\nThat is the idea behind The Sound Above.\n\nThis is a chance to explain what I am making, what I hope to explore, and how you can follow along.\n\n[FREEFORM: What brought this series back to mind now? Use a specific moment, archive task, or question that is true today.]",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. Archive page or interview still is optional.]\nThe Sound Above is a series about revisiting conversations from the UGtastic and WHOIS Tech Community interview archive.\n\nI want to listen to what people said in their own words, put the recording back in its time and place, and ask what those ideas mean to people building software today.\n\nThe archive is the source. My memories and present-day reactions are part of the conversation, but they are not a substitute for what the guest said.\n\n[FREEFORM: Describe one thing you noticed while working with the archive. Avoid naming a guest or making a historical claim unless you can point to its source.]",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. Keep this personal and specific.]\nI care about these recordings because technical communities are made by people showing up for each other. The interviews preserve some of those moments: what people were trying, how they learned, and how they made room for someone else to participate.\n\nI also want to see what I remember clearly and what the recording helps me notice again. That can be a useful kind of history. It leaves room for correction and for other people to remember the same time differently.\n\n[FREEFORM: Share one memory that belongs to you. Mark it as your recollection. Leave space for the guest's account and other people's memories.]",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. A simple season outline may appear on screen.]\nI have a working Season One target of seventeen interviews. The first one in the current sequence is my conversation with Sergio Pereira about Chicago Alt.NET.\n\nThat is a planning target, not a release schedule. I want to make the first episodes, learn what works, and adjust the format as I go.\n\nThe episodes can connect an interview to its community, conference, and time. Some may include transcripts or other archive material. I will decide what belongs in each one as I prepare it.\n\n[FREEFORM: Name the next topic only if the sequence is still current when recording. Do not promise a date or cadence.]",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. Invite a concrete response.]\nIf you were part of one of these communities, I would like to hear what you remember. Which room, meetup, conversation, or person helped you find your place in software?\n\nYou can leave a comment. If you remember a detail differently, say so. A specific memory or a source link will help me follow up carefully.\n\nI am building this as a public record. I want it to be useful to people who were there and to people discovering these stories now.",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. Keep the invitation direct and low pressure.]\nThe simplest way to follow the series is to subscribe to the YouTube channel. That is free, and it helps you find the next video when it is ready.\n\nPatreon is one way to support the time I spend recording, editing, caring for the interview archive, and making software. I share occasional notes about that work and host our Hallway community chat. I do not have a fixed posting schedule, and membership will not unlock private recordings or change what I choose to make public.\n\nIf you would like to support the work, the Patreon link is in the description. Please join only if the offer makes sense for you. The public videos and interviews will remain available on their usual sites.",
  "[SCENE: Host camera. No forced sign-off needed.]\nThat is what I am trying to make with The Sound Above: a way to listen again, add context carefully, and keep these conversations available to people who care about them.\n\nSubscribe if you would like to follow along. And if you were part of one of these communities, tell me what you remember.\n\nThanks for spending some time with me. I will see you in the next one."
]
